course: everyday
lesson: 393
title: Bystanders: the crowd, the phone and the ones who help
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Behind the mobile classroom, Jake and a Station Road boy squared up, and within a minute a crowd had formed, chanting «fight, fight, fight». Sam stood at the back with his stomach in knots, not knowing what to do. A crowd is like petrol: neither boy wanted to back down with thirty faces watching. Some kids had their phones out, filming. Lily marched up to them and said «stop it — would you want that video of you?», and two of them actually put their phones away.
explain: Maddie, who knows more about fights than most, did something surprising: she turned to the crowd, not the fighters, and said «go away, show's over, you're making it worse». As the crowd thinned, the two boys had no one to perform for, and the shoving slowed. Meanwhile Sam and Freddie had run, together, to fetch the teacher on duty — going in a pair meant neither of them chickened out on the way.
explain: Kieran, at a different fight, laughed and cheered. Later he found out the boy had hit his head on the kerb and spent the night in hospital. Kieran couldn't sleep, and the next day he went quiet in a way nobody had seen. Laughing had felt like nothing at the time; afterwards it felt like being part of it. Watchers who cheer are not just watchers.
explain: Bystanders have lots of better jobs. Chloe, who usually keeps away from Maddie, saw her clench her fists at a boy and said quietly «don't, Maddie» — and Maddie stopped. Lily and Sam helped up a girl who had been pushed over and stayed with her until she stopped shaking. And Jake, who used to start fights, stepped between two Year 4s and said «oi, pack it in, not worth it» — and because it was Jake, they did. He walked off looking secretly proud.
fact: A bystander is someone who sees something happening to other people.
fact: A crowd watching a fight makes it harder for the fighters to stop, because nobody wants to lose face.
fact: Chanting or cheering at a fight encourages it and makes the cheering people part of it.
fact: Filming a fight and sharing it can hurt and shame people, and can get the filmer in trouble.
fact: Fetching an adult quickly is one of the most useful things a bystander can do.
fact: Going for help in a pair is easier and makes it more likely you will actually do it.
fact: A blow or a fall onto a hard surface like a kerb can cause a serious head injury.
fact: Checking on someone who has been hurt or pushed, and staying with them, really helps them.
fact: A calm word from a friend can stop someone before a fight starts.
fact: Children should not step between two people who are actually hitting each other; getting an adult is safer.
fact: Older children whom others respect can often calm younger ones with words alone.
exercise: Why did the crowd make the fight worse? | Neither boy wanted to back down in front of everyone | The crowd joined in the punching and made it much worse | The crowd told them to stop | The crowd went to get a teacher
exercise: How did Sam feel watching the fight? | Knotted up and unsure what to do | Excited and cheering along with the others | Bored and sleepy | Angry at the teacher
exercise: What did Lily tell the kids filming? | «Stop — would you want that video of you?» | «Send it to me later, I want to see it again» | «Film from the other side» | «Make sure you get the punch»
exercise: Who did Maddie talk to at the fight? | The crowd, telling them to go away | The fighters, telling them who would win | The teacher on duty, asking to join in | Nobody, she just stood and watched it all
exercise: Why did sending the crowd away help? | The fighters had no one to perform for | The crowd took the two fighters away with them | The fighters ran after the crowd to the shop | It made the fight more fun
exercise: Why did Sam and Freddie go together for the teacher? | So neither would chicken out on the way | So they could run faster all the way to the office | So nobody would believe them | Because the school rules say pairs only
exercise: What happened to the boy at the fight Kieran laughed at? | He hit his head on the kerb and went to hospital | He walked off laughing with his friends | He got a prize for winning the fight in the end, from the older boys | Nothing at all happened
exercise: How did Kieran feel afterwards? | Guilty and quiet, as if he had been part of it | Proud that he had cheered louder than anyone else | Happy that it was not him | Bored by all the fuss
exercise: Why are cheerers «not just watchers»? | Cheering pushes the fight on | Cheerers end up throwing punches too | Cheerers get injured most of all | Cheerers are the ones who start every fight
exercise: What did Chloe say when Maddie clenched her fists? | «Don't, Maddie» | «Go on, Maddie, get him» | «Hit him harder» | «I'm telling on you»
exercise: Why was Chloe's word brave? | She usually keeps away from Maddie | Chloe loves fighting more than anything | Maddie never listens to anyone at all | Chloe was bigger and stronger than Maddie
exercise: What did Lily and Sam do for the girl who was pushed? | Helped her up and stayed until she stopped shaking | Chased the boy who pushed her all the way down the road | Took a photo of her knee | Told her to stop crying
exercise: How did Jake stop the Year 4s? | He said «pack it in, not worth it» | He grabbed one of them by the arm and pulled | He hit the bigger one | He filmed it for later
exercise: Why did the Year 4s listen to Jake? | They respected him because he was Jake | Jake paid them in sweets to stop it | They thought he was a teacher from the big school | Jake threatened them
exercise: Two kids are actually hitting each other. What is the safest thing for a child bystander? | Get an adult fast | Jump in between them | Throw water on them | Hold one of them down
exercise: Which is NOT a helpful bystander job? | Filming the fight to share | Fetching a teacher | Checking on the hurt one | Telling the crowd to go
test: crowd: «FIGHT FIGHT FIGHT». you're at the back. best move? | Walk away and get a teacher | Push to the front to see better | Chant louder so they keep going | Start filming
test: «lol got it all on my phone, sending to the group». What's true? | Sharing it can shame people and land you in trouble | It's fine, it's just a video, everyone does it | It proves who won | Teachers love those videos
test: Kieran: «I only laughed, I didn't do nothing». Is that right? | Laughing and cheering helped push the fight on | Yes, laughing is just watching, not fighting | Yes, only punches count | Laughing stops fights
test: Freddie: «I'm not going on me own to get sir». What can Sam do? | Go with him, so they both get there | Tell him to stay and watch it with you | Go film it instead | Say «chicken» and stay
test: Maddie to the crowd: «show's over, get lost». Why might it work? | Fighters calm down when nobody's watching | The crowd will turn round and fight Maddie instead | Crowds always obey Maddie | It makes the fighters angrier
test: «don't, Maddie» — it's Chloe, who never talks to her. Maddie stops. What made it work? | A quiet word from someone unexpected | Chloe threatened her | Maddie was tired from running about | Chloe called a teacher over
test: girl pushed over, everyone's walked off. What's kind and useful? | Help her up, ask if she's OK, stay or get an adult | Laugh so she doesn't feel bad | Leave her, it's not your fight, someone else will help | Find the pusher and push back
test: two Year 4s scrapping, you're a big Year 6 they look up to. Best? | Say «pack it in» calmly, and get a teacher if they keep going | Pick a side and help your mate win | Grab them both by the collar and drag them apart yourself, quick | Film it to show their mums
test: boy on the ground holding his head after falling on the kerb. What's right? | Get an adult straight away; head injuries can be serious | Help him up and carry on playing | Tell him to walk it off and have a drink of water, it's only a bump | Leave him, he'll be fine in a minute
test: «not my business, I just watch». Which is true? | Bystanders can stop or worsen a fight | Bystanders change nothing, whatever they do | Only teachers can make a difference | Watching is always harmless
test: teacher asks: «Who saw what happened behind the mobile?» You did. Best? | Say what you saw, nothing more | Say nothing, it's snitching | Blame the Station Road boy for all of it | Make it sound worse
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a gathering of onlookers tends to prolong a physical altercation. | The participants are reluctant to withdraw before an audience | The onlookers invariably join the altercation themselves | The onlookers summon assistance too promptly | An audience has no bearing upon the participants whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recording and dissemination of a fight by means of a mobile telephone? | It may humiliate those involved and render the person recording liable to sanction | It is a neutral act carrying no consequence for anyone | It is a recommended method of reporting incidents to a member of staff | It brings the fight to a swifter conclusion
exam: Whereas two pupils are exchanging blows, select the response of a child bystander most consistent with established guidance concerning safety. | The prompt summoning of a responsible adult | Physical intervention between the combatants | Encouragement of the more deserving party to the dispute | The capture of video evidence at close range
exam: It is given that a pupil was injured upon striking his head against a kerb. State the conclusion consistent with the established facts concerning bystanders who cheered. | Their encouragement formed part of the conduct that led to the harm | They bear no connection whatever to the harm that occurred | They acted as neutral observers only | They are exempt from blame because they did not themselves strike a blow
